# 5. AREA THREE – WATERFALL LAGOON & WATERWORKS

## Location

Western side of the Main Mine Hall.

The waterfall area must connect naturally to the main hall and must not become a disconnected room.

### A. Waterfall

Create a large natural waterfall descending from a high cave wall.

Include:
- A tall rocky cliff.
- A strong waterfall.
- A turquoise-blue lagoon.
- Natural stones around the water.
- Small glowing blue crystals.
- A stone walkway.
- A small stone bridge.
- Water reflections and atmospheric lighting.

The lagoon should look like a natural underground water source.

### B. Water channels

This is a major environmental feature.

The water must continue beyond the lagoon.

Build an actual water system that carries water toward the lower underground levels.

Include:
- Carved stone aqueducts.
- Water channels.
- Narrow water passages.
- Small waterfalls.
- Stone water conduits.
- Iron pipes where appropriate.

Water should flow in a believable direction, following elevation differences.

### C. Mechanical water wheel

Construct a large dwarven water wheel near the lower section of the water system.

It should have:
- A large wooden or metal wheel.
- Stone supports.
- Mechanical gears.
- Iron axles.
- Pipes and connecting mechanisms.

The water wheel should appear to power an industrial mechanism, such as an ore crusher or lifting machine.

### D. Lower waterfall connection

The water system should continue into the Grand Chasm area.

Create at least one visible waterfall descending into the lower cavern.

This will connect the upper and lower environments visually.

### Important design rule

The water must appear to be one continuous environmental system, not several unrelated pools.

