# 6. AREA FOUR – MINING GALLERIES

## Location

Eastern side of the Main Mine Hall.

This area must look like a real working mine.

The player should immediately understand that this is where the dwarves extract raw materials from the mountain.

### A. Mining structure

Use several natural rock excavation areas.

Include:
- Exposed mineral veins.
- Deeply excavated rock walls.
- Different mining elevations.
- Wooden support structures.
- Scaffolding.
- Ladders.
- Small platforms.
- Mining tunnels.
- Railway tracks.

The architecture should be irregular and natural.

### B. Mineral deposits

Add different mineral types.

Examples:
- Iron.
- Copper.
- Gold.
- Blue crystals.
- Rare purple crystals.
- Dark mineral deposits.

Minerals should visibly emerge from the surrounding rock.

Do not place all minerals as separate glowing objects on top of the terrain.

They should look embedded into the rock formations.

### C. Mining activities

Include:
- Dwarves mining with pickaxes.
- Workers collecting extracted stones.
- Small mining carts.
- Stone piles.
- Ore containers.
- Storage areas.
- Work platforms.

### D. Machinery

Add a large stone-crushing mechanism.

It can include:
- Mechanical pistons.
- Rotating gears.
- Crushing plates.
- Ore collection containers.

Add an ore-lifting system using chains and pulleys.

### E. Multiple elevations

The mining galleries should not be flat.

Create:
- Lower mining tunnels.
- Raised mining platforms.
- Staircases.
- Wooden bridges.
- Small elevated work areas.

All areas intended for exploration must have accessible routes.

