# 18. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION AND PERFORMANCE

Build the environment using modular, reusable components.

Avoid creating excessive independent objects for repeated environmental assets.

Use appropriate optimization techniques for the existing game engine.

### Repeated assets

Examples:
- Torches.
- Barrels.
- Crates.
- Stone pillars.
- Chains.
- Mining carts.
- Repeated rock details.
- Repeated wall decorations.

Use instancing, batching or equivalent techniques where supported and appropriate.

### Static geometry

- Optimize static structures.
- Use appropriate collision meshes.
- Avoid unnecessary complex colliders.
- Separate decorative objects from gameplay collision.
- Reuse modular assets.
- Use level-of-detail techniques where appropriate.

### Lighting

Avoid unnecessarily expensive dynamic lights on every small object.

Use static or baked lighting where appropriate, and reserve dynamic lighting for important elements such as moving machinery, lava and selected torches.

### Environmental organization

Organize the mine into logical sections or chunks.

Separate the upper industrial level, lower cavern and deep treasure area where appropriate.

Make sure optimization does not break lighting continuity or visible connections between sections.

Do not sacrifice visual quality unnecessarily.

