# 20. FINAL QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

The work is considered complete only when the following requirements are satisfied:

1. The mine exists as an actual playable 3D environment.
2. The main areas are physically interconnected.
3. The upper mine feels like an active industrial settlement.
4. The waterfall system visibly continues between elevations.
5. The Mining Galleries look like real excavation areas.
6. The Smelting Foundry is integrated into the industrial section.
7. The lower cavern feels enormous.
8. The Heroes Hall is connected to the main cavern.
9. The Great Bridge has believable architecture and structural support.
10. The Golem Guardian Chamber connects to the Dragon Forge.
11. The dragon-head furnace pours lava into the final chamber.
12. The lava stepping stones are actually traversable.
13. The treasure chest is reachable.
14. All main pathways have proper collision and navigation.
15. The environment maintains a consistent dwarven architectural identity.
16. The environment is appropriately optimized for the existing game.

# FINAL DIRECTION

The most important objective is to create a believable ancient dwarven kingdom carved into an enormous underground mountain.

Do not interpret this project as a simple dungeon with several rooms.

Think of it as a complete underground civilization:

A place where dwarves mine, transport, process and smelt minerals; a place with an engineered water system; a place with ancient monuments and royal memorials; and, deeper underground, a mysterious world containing an enormous abyss, a guardian and a legendary treasure.

Architectural quality, believable connections, scale, environmental storytelling and actual gameplay functionality must take priority over simply adding more objects.

Build a world that feels like it has existed for centuries and that the player is discovering for the first time.